  "text": "Rain is a fundamental part of Earth\u2019s natural water cycle, a continuous process that circulates water between the planet\u2019s surface and the atmosphere. Understanding how rain forms and how this cycle sustains life requires looking at the interconnected stages of evaporation, condensation, and precipitation.\n\nThe process begins with evaporation. The sun\u2019s energy heats up water in oceans, lakes, rivers, and even from the soil and plants. As this water warms, it changes from a liquid state into water vapor, an invisible gas. This vapor rises into the atmosphere because it is lighter than the surrounding air. As the vapor ascends, it enters cooler regions of the atmosphere. When the temperature drops, the water vapor cools and changes back into tiny liquid droplets or ice crystals. This process is called condensation. These microscopic droplets cluster around tiny particles of dust or pollen in the air, forming clouds.\n\nAs more water vapor condenses, the droplets within the cloud grow larger and heavier. Eventually, gravity pulls these droplets down to the Earth\u2019s surface. This falling water is precipitation, which we experience as rain, snow, sleet, or hail, depending on the temperature. When rain falls, it replenishes the planet\u2019s fresh water supply.\n\nThis",
